@namespace Talk.Client.Slides

@* ErrorBoundary — a component you wrap around other components so a throw degrades to a fallback
   instead of taking down the app. Dogfooded: the code is (trimmed) the real wrap around this
   deck's own slide host in Deck.razor. The next slide (D3cBoom) breaks a real slide live.
   Notes below the code, not beside it: the SlideErrorFallback line is 79 chars and clipped badly
   in a SplitSlide column (.cs-pre clips rather than wraps). It's also re-wrapped onto two lines —
   full width alone isn't enough, because CodeWindow's font is sized in cqw and grows with the
   container, so a wider column buys much less capacity than it looks like it should. *@
<Slide Title="ErrorBoundary" Subtitle="Wrap components so a throw doesn't take down the app">
    <div class="eb-body">
        <CodeWindow FileName="Deck.razor" Language="razor" Code="@Wrap" />

        <Caption>Wrap a subtree in <code>&lt;ErrorBoundary&gt;</code> and a throw inside it — in
            render or in a lifecycle method — shows <strong>ErrorContent</strong> instead of tearing
            down the circuit. Two slots: <strong>ChildContent</strong> for the normal UI, and
            <strong>ErrorContent</strong>, handed the exception.</Caption>

        <Caption Spaced>This deck keys every slide, so each gets a fresh boundary — navigate away and
            it recovers on its own, and <code>Recover()</code> re-attempts the same slide in
            place.</Caption>
    </div>
</Slide>

@code {
    private const string Wrap = @"<ErrorBoundary @ref=""_boundary"">
    <ChildContent>
        <DynamicComponent Type=""Slides[State.Index].Type"" />
    </ChildContent>
    <ErrorContent Context=""ex"">
        <SlideErrorFallback Error=""ex""
                            OnRecover=""() => _boundary.Recover()"" />
    </ErrorContent>
</ErrorBoundary>";
}
